    "summary": "Smoking Dark Star, tight Attics Of My Life, high‑energy late‑72 set",
    "blurb": "The show delivers a searing Dark Star and a focused Attics Of My Life, with Phil delivering a thunderous \"Philo Stomp\" throughout. Jerry’s lead work shines on the jams despite occasional mix burying, and the band rides a consistently high energy level.",
    "review": "October 28, 1972 at Cleveland Public Hall captures the Dead in full stride during their late‑'72 run. The first set rolls through upbeat numbers before launching into an expansive second half where Dark Star stretches close to thirty minutes, anchored by a muscular Phil Lesh and Bill Kreutzmann that keep the groove tight even as the mix leans heavy on drums. Attics Of My Life stands out for its lyrical depth and restrained piano from Keith, while the closing Sugar Magnolia rounds out a set that never loses its momentum. Though some tapes suffer from uneven balances—Jerry’s guitar can dip into the background and the kick drum occasionally overpowers the mix—the overall performance is vibrant, with highlights like Greatest Story Ever Told and He's Gone showcasing the band’s improvisational chemistry. The finny‑sourced soundboard capture offers the clearest window onto this high‑octane night.",
